window.CYTRON_ARTICLES={"esp32-smart-home-dashboard-with-real-time-sensor-data": {"hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/esp32-smart-home-dashboard-with-real-time-sensor-d/esp32-smart-home-dashboard-with-real-time-sensor-data.png", "body": "<p></p><h2>Introduction</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In this tutorial, you will create a smart home environmental monitoring dashboard hosted directly on the <a href=\"/p-maker-esp32\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Maker ESP32</a>. The system collects real-time temperature and humidity readings using a DHT11 sensor connected via the onboard Maker Port. Any smartphone, tablet, or computer connected to the same Wi-Fi network can open the web dashboard to view live sensor updates without installing any mobile app.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><h2>Disclaimer / Safety Notes</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This project is an educational prototype designed for learning and experimentation. It is intended for indoor temperature and humidity monitoring and should not be relied upon for critical climate control or life-safety applications.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><h2>Prerequisites</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Before starting, make sure your <a href=\"/p-maker-esp32\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Maker ESP32</a> is ready to program. If this is your first time using the board, follow the <a href=\"/tutorial/getting-started-with-maker-esp32\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Maker ESP32 Getting Started guide</a> first.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><h2>Objectives</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective of this project is to build an environmental monitoring dashboard hosted on the <a href=\"/p-maker-esp32\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Maker ESP32</a>. You will connect the DHT11 sensor to the microcontroller using the onboard Maker Port, read temperature and humidity levels, and serve an interactive, auto-refreshing web dashboard over your local Wi-Fi network.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><h2>List of Components / BOM</h2><ol><li><a href=\"/p-maker-esp32\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Maker ESP32</a> x1</li><li><a href=\"/p-crowtail-temperature-and-humidity-sensor-2.0\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">DHT 11</a> x1</li><li><a href=\"/p-grove-to-jst-sh-qwiic-cable-20cm\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Grove to JST-SH (Qwiic) Cable - 20cm</a> x1</li></ol><p>&nbsp;</p><h2>System Diagram &amp; Wiring</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Connect the DHT11 sensor to the <a href=\"/p-maker-esp32\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Maker ESP32</a> using the plug-and-play Maker Port as detailed in the wiring table below.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><table><thead><tr><th>DHT11 Pin</th><th>Maker ESP32 Maker Port Pin</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>VCC</td><td>3.3V (Pin 2)</td><td>3.3V Power Supply</td></tr><tr><td>GND</td><td>GND (Pin 1)</td><td>Ground</td></tr><tr><td>Signal / Data</td><td>GPIO21 / SDA (Pin 3)</td><td>Digital Temperature &amp; Humidity Data</td></tr></tbody></table><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><em>Note: The <a href=\"/p-maker-esp32\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Maker ESP32</a> Maker Port provides 3.3V power, ground, and connects the sensor data line directly to GPIO21 (SDA). If you are using a standard 3-pin DHT11 module with male header pins instead of a Crowtail/Grove module, use the <a href=\"/p-stemmaqt-qwiic-jst-sh-4-pin-cable-with-premium-female-sockets-150mm\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">STEMMA QT / Qwiic JST SH 4-pin Cable with Premium Female Sockets 150mm</a>.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><h2>Software Setup</h2><h3>Install DHT Sensor Library</h3><ol><li>Open the Arduino IDE.</li><li>Navigate to <strong>Tools</strong> -&gt; <strong>Manage Libraries...</strong>.</li><li>Search for <strong>DHT sensor library</strong> by <strong>Adafruit</strong>.</li><li>Click <strong>Install</strong>. When prompted to install missing dependencies (such as <strong>Adafruit Unified Sensor</strong>), click <strong>Install All</strong>.</li></ol><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><em>Note: The <em>WiFi.h</em> and <em>WebServer.h</em> libraries are included automatically with the ESP32 Arduino core and do not require separate installation.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><h2>Sample Code</h2><p>&nbsp;</p> <p>&nbsp;</p><h3>Key Code Explanation</h3><ul><li><em>#define DHTPIN 21</em>: Assigns GPIO21 (Maker Port SDA) to the DHT11 digital signal line on the <a href=\"/p-maker-esp32\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Maker ESP32</a>.</li><li><em>WebServer server(80)</em>: Instantiates an HTTP web server listening on standard port 80.</li><li><em>server.on(\"/\", handleRoot)</em>: Delivers the stored HTML dashboard to connected web browsers.</li><li><em>server.on(\"/data\", handleData)</em>: Returns live temperature and humidity readings as a JSON object to refresh the dashboard without reloading the page.</li><li><em>server.handleClient()</em>: Keeps the web server listening and processing client requests continuously in the loop.</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><h2>Testing &amp; Validation</h2><ol><li>Connect your <a href=\"/p-maker-esp32\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Maker ESP32</a> to your computer using a USB-C cable.</li><li>In the Arduino IDE, update <em>YOUR_WIFI_SSID</em> and <em>YOUR_WIFI_PASSWORD</em> with your local 2.4GHz Wi-Fi credentials.</li><li>Select your board and COM port, then click <strong>Upload</strong>.</li><li>Open the <strong>Serial Monitor</strong> from <strong>Tools</strong> -&gt; <strong>Serial Monitor</strong> and set the baud rate to <strong>115200</strong>.</li><li>Note the assigned IP address shown in the Serial Monitor.</li><li>Open any web browser on a smartphone, tablet, or PC connected to the same Wi-Fi network, and type the IP address into the URL bar.</li></ol><h3>Expected Results</h3><ul><li>The Serial Monitor displays the Wi-Fi connection confirmation, local IP address, and continuous sensor readings.</li><li>The web browser renders the Smart Home Dashboard with live temperature and humidity cards that update every 2 seconds.</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><h2>Demo / Results</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When your <a href=\"/p-maker-esp32\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Maker ESP32</a> boots and connects to your local network, the Serial Monitor outputs:</p><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Connecting to Wi-Fi..... Wi-Fi connected. IP address: 192.168.1.50 HTTP server started. Temperature: 28.0 °C | Humidity: 65.0 %</code></pre><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Opening <em>http://192.168.1.50</em> in a web browser loads the responsive Smart Home Dashboard. The cards for Temperature and Humidity update automatically in real time without refreshing the page.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><h2>Troubleshooting &amp; Extra Tips</h2><h3>Wi-Fi Does Not Connect (Continuous Dots in Serial Monitor)</h3><ul><li>Verify that your router broadcasts a 2.4GHz network; the <a href=\"/p-maker-esp32\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Maker ESP32</a> does not support 5GHz Wi-Fi.</li><li>Double-check your SSID and password for typos or capitalization errors.</li></ul><h3>Dashboard Does Not Load in Browser</h3><ul><li>Ensure your phone or computer is connected to the exact same local Wi-Fi network as your <a href=\"/p-maker-esp32\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Maker ESP32</a>.</li><li>Confirm you typed the correct IP address displayed in the Serial Monitor.</li></ul><h3>DHT11 Displays 0.0 or Fails to Read</h3><ul><li>Check that the DHT11 cable is firmly connected to the Maker Port on the <a href=\"/p-maker-esp32\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Maker ESP32</a> and ensure the data line corresponds to GPIO21.</li><li>Verify that the sensor is not loose.</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: center;\"><a href=\"https://t.me/ESPmakersMY\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/esp32-smart-home-dashboard-with-real-time-sensor-d/telegram-banner-esp32-footer.png \" style=\"width: 900px; height: 253px;\"></a></p><p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;</p><p></p>", "products": [{"name": "Crowtail - DHT11 Temperature & Humidity Sensor (Grove Compatible)", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-crowtail-temperature-and-humidity-sensor-2.0", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/CWT-DHT11/cwt-dht11-a-268x268.png", "price": "RM9.90", "oldPrice": null, "qty": "x 1 unit(s)", "stock": null}, {"name": "Maker ESP32", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-maker-esp32", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/MAKER-ESP32/maker-esp32-a-268x268.png", "price": "RM32.76", "oldPrice": "RM40.95", "qty": "x 1 unit(s)", "stock": "Coming Soon"}]}, "mddrc5-500g-rc-sumo-rovation-2026": {"hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/mddrc5-500g-rc-sumo-rovation-2026/success-story-mddrc5.png", "body": "<p></p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Introduction</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When developing a new product, testing it on the bench is important. But sometimes, the best way to understand how it performs is to put it into the environment it was actually designed for. That was exactly what we wanted to do with our new MDDRC5 RC motor driver.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of stopping at internal testing, the Cytron Team decided to build a <strong>500g RC Sumo Robot</strong> around the MDDRC5 and enter it into an actual competition. The destination was <strong>ROVATION 2026</strong>, held at <strong>Kolej Vokasional Kulim from 20–22 July 2026</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Our objective was simple:&nbsp;<strong>Put MDDRC5 into a real robot, compete under actual battle conditions, and see how it performs.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">We went there to test a product. We came back as&nbsp;Champion!</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/mddrc5-500g-rc-sumo-rovation-2026/photo-6312325650278191728-y.jpg \" style=\"width: 800px; height: 1067px;\"></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why Take MDDRC5 Into a Competition?</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">There is only so much we can learn from testing a motor driver on a workbench. A motor driver might operate perfectly when the motor is running without much load, but a robot competition creates a completely different environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In an RC Sumo match, the robot has to react quickly to the driver's commands. It needs to accelerate, reverse, turn and push against another robot while trying to stay inside the arena. For the motor driver, this means it has to handle repeated and rapidly changing motor demands throughout the match. That makes an actual Sumo competition a useful real-world test for a new RC motor driver.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of creating an artificial test condition, we decided:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why not put MDDRC5 into the application it was designed for and compete with it?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Meet Our 500g RC Sumo Robot</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For ROVATION 2026, the Cytron team prepared a compact robot for the <strong>Robot Sumo RC 500G Open</strong> category. The robot was designed around a straightforward goal: stay compact, respond quickly and generate enough pushing force to fight another robot inside the Sumo arena. At the front of the robot is a metal wedge designed to get underneath the opponent, while the drive system provides the movement required for attacking, turning and defending.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the centre of the electrical system is the <strong>MDDRC5</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The MDDRC5 controls the robot's two drive motors based on commands from the RC system, allowing the driver to control the movement of the robot directly during the match. For us, this robot was more than a competition entry. It was also a working test platform for the new motor driver.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/mddrc5-500g-rc-sumo-rovation-2026/new-robotcloseup.jpg \" style=\"width: 800px; height: 822px;\"></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">From Testing to the Competition Ring</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">ROVATION, or <strong>“Powering Possibilities”</strong>, was organised at Kolej Vokasional Kulim as part of a national-level robotics, innovation, drone and entrepreneurship event. Among the competitions conducted during the event was the <strong>Robot Sumo RC 500G Open</strong> category. This gave us exactly the environment we were looking for.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Once the robot entered the arena, there was no longer a controlled laboratory setup. The robot had to respond to actual RC commands while facing another robot that was actively trying to push it out of the ring. Every match became another opportunity for us to observe how the complete system behaved.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>RC Controller → MDDRC5 → Motors → Robot Movement → Sumo Battle</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">More importantly, we were no longer evaluating MDDRC5 as an isolated electronic board. We were testing it as part of a complete working robot.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">And Then... We Won</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The original goal was to test MDDRC5. Winning the competition was a very welcome bonus. After going through the matches, the Cytron team's 500g RC Sumo Robot finished <strong>first place in the Robot Sumo RC 500G Open category</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The team received the Champion&nbsp;award together with medals, a certificate of achievement and <strong>RM400 prize money</strong>. For us, however, the most valuable outcome was not just the medal. It was seeing a newly developed product move from internal development into an actual competition robot — and successfully perform all the way to a championship result.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">More Than Just a Trophy</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Winning ROVATION gave us a success story, but more importantly, it gave the engineering team greater confidence in the product. Bench testing helps us understand specifications and electrical behaviour. A real application tells us something different.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It shows how the product behaves when it becomes part of an actual system. In this case, MDDRC5 was not sitting on a test fixture. It was installed inside a compact 500g robot, connected to actual motors and operated repeatedly during a competitive event.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That is the kind of testing that is difficult to reproduce completely on a workbench. And that is why this competition mattered to us.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Built to Be Used, Not Just Tested</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At Cytron, we develop motor drivers for people who actually build things. That includes students, makers, educators, engineers and robot builders. So when we develop a new motor driver, we also want to experience the product from the builder's point of view.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How does it fit into the robot?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How straightforward is the connection?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How does the robot respond?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">And most importantly:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Would we be confident using it ourselves in a competition?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For MDDRC5, ROVATION 2026 helped us answer that question. Yes. And we have a championship medal to remember the test by.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/mddrc5-500g-rc-sumo-rovation-2026/awardceremony.jpg \" style=\"width: 800px; height: 755px;\"></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Conclusion</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The journey of MDDRC5 did not stop after development and internal testing. We took it out of the lab, installed it into a real 500g RC Sumo Robot and put it into competition.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Our main objective was product validation. The result was <strong>first place</strong>. Of course, one competition does not represent every possible application or operating condition. But for us, ROVATION 2026 became an important milestone in the development story of MDDRC5.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It showed us something that specifications alone cannot show:&nbsp;<strong>The product could become part of a real robot, enter a real competition and get the job done.</strong>&nbsp;From the test bench to the Sumo ring — that was one test we will definitely remember.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: center;\"><a href=\"http://qr.codes/YO63x0 \" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/mddrc5-500g-rc-sumo-rovation-2026/sumo-drone-stem-robotik.png \" style=\"width: 900px; height: 200px;\"></a></p><p></p>", "products": [{"name": "5 Amp 7V-30V DC Motor Driver for R/C (2 Channels)", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-5-amp-7v-30v-dc-motor-driver-for-r-c-2-channels", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/MDDRC5/mddrc5-a1-268x268.png", "price": "RM126.00", "oldPrice": null, "qty": "x 1 unit(s)", "stock": null}]}, "real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi": {"hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi/real-time-ppe-detection-on-raspberry-pi-with-edge-ai.png", "body": "<p></p><h2 data-end=\"1634\" data-section-id=\"13ax1s5\" data-start=\"1619\">Introduction</h2><p data-end=\"1997\" data-start=\"1636\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) is one of the most important safety requirements on construction sites, in factories, warehouses, and other hazardous work environments. Helmets, safety vests, and other protective equipment may look simple, but ensuring every worker is properly equipped before entering a restricted area still requires consistent checks.</p><p data-end=\"1997\" data-start=\"1636\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"2050\" data-start=\"1999\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is where edge AI can make the process smarter.</p><p data-end=\"2050\" data-start=\"1999\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"2419\" data-start=\"2052\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">By combining a camera, Raspberry Pi and a dedicated AI accelerator, PPE compliance can be detected automatically from the camera feed. Instead of checking workers one by one, the system identifies protective equipment such as helmets and safety vests, places bounding boxes around each detection and displays a confidence score together with an overall safety status.</p><p data-end=\"2840\" data-start=\"2650\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"2840\" data-start=\"2650\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">In one detection scene, multiple workers are analysed at the same time. Helmet and vest detections appear individually while a green <strong data-end=\"2796\" data-start=\"2783\">SAFETY OK</strong> indicator provides a simple overall status.</p><p data-end=\"2986\" data-start=\"2842\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"2986\" data-start=\"2842\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">This turns several individual AI detections into information that can be understood quickly — an important step toward automated PPE monitoring.</p><p data-end=\"3016\" data-section-id=\"poqgip\" data-start=\"2988\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"3016\" data-section-id=\"poqgip\" data-start=\"2988\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><lite-youtube videoid=\"DYnWKGp1a4U\"></lite-youtube></p><p data-end=\"3016\" data-section-id=\"poqgip\" data-start=\"2988\">&nbsp;</p><h2 data-end=\"3016\" data-section-id=\"poqgip\" data-start=\"2988\">Why PPE Detection Matters</h2><p data-end=\"3162\" data-start=\"3018\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional PPE checking normally depends on supervisors or safety personnel visually inspecting workers at an entrance or around the work area.</p><p data-end=\"3162\" data-start=\"3018\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"3345\" data-start=\"3164\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">For a small team, that may be manageable. As the number of workers, entrances or monitored areas increases, however, manual checking becomes more difficult to maintain consistently.</p><p data-end=\"3345\" data-start=\"3164\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"3389\" data-start=\"3347\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Computer vision provides another approach.</p><p data-end=\"3389\" data-start=\"3347\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"3680\" data-start=\"3391\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">A camera can monitor a defined work area while an AI model analyses the image for the required PPE classes. Instead of requiring a supervisor to inspect every worker individually, the system can highlight potential compliance conditions automatically and present the information on screen.</p><p data-end=\"3884\" data-start=\"3682\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"3884\" data-start=\"3682\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">For this project, the main detection targets are <strong data-end=\"3759\" data-start=\"3731\">helmets and safety vests</strong>. Gloves are also part of the intended PPE requirement, although glove detection is not included in the demonstrated results.</p><p data-end=\"4090\" data-start=\"3886\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"4090\" data-start=\"3886\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">The important idea is not simply detecting an object. It is turning visual information from a work area into useful safety information that can eventually become part of a larger industrial safety system.</p><p data-end=\"4129\" data-section-id=\"3a7g0h\" data-start=\"4092\">&nbsp;</p><h2 data-end=\"4129\" data-section-id=\"3a7g0h\" data-start=\"4092\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">How the PPE Detection System Works</h2><p data-end=\"4185\" data-start=\"4131\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">The system follows a straightforward edge AI workflow:</p><p data-end=\"4262\" data-start=\"4187\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi/blockdiagram-ppe1.png \" style=\"width: 800px; height: 126px;\"></p><p data-end=\"4452\" data-start=\"4264\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">A camera first captures the working area. The Raspberry Pi acts as the main computing platform, while the Raspberry Pi AI HAT+ provides dedicated hardware acceleration for the AI workload.</p><p data-end=\"4711\" data-start=\"4454\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"4711\" data-start=\"4454\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">The detection model analyses each incoming frame and identifies the PPE classes it has been trained to recognise. When an object is detected, the interface draws a bounding box around it and displays the corresponding class together with a confidence score.</p><p data-end=\"4725\" data-start=\"4713\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"4725\" data-start=\"4713\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example:</p><ul data-end=\"4776\" data-start=\"4727\"><li data-end=\"4744\" data-section-id=\"1v1u7t0\" data-start=\"4727\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong data-end=\"4744\" data-start=\"4729\">Helmet 0.78</strong></li><li data-end=\"4760\" data-section-id=\"1k0vlb5\" data-start=\"4745\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong data-end=\"4760\" data-start=\"4747\">Vest 0.74</strong></li><li data-end=\"4776\" data-section-id=\"19x4g0t\" data-start=\"4761\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong data-end=\"4776\" data-start=\"4763\">Head 0.47</strong></li></ul><p data-end=\"4938\" data-start=\"4778\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"4938\" data-start=\"4778\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">These values represent the confidence of each detection. They should not be interpreted as the overall accuracy of the complete PPE detection system.</p><p data-end=\"5071\" data-start=\"4940\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi/blockdiagram-ppe2.png \" style=\"width: 800px; height: 100px;\"></p><p data-end=\"5368\" data-start=\"5155\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Keeping the AI processing close to the camera is what makes this an <strong data-end=\"5234\" data-start=\"5223\">edge AI</strong> application. The image can be analysed locally rather than requiring the core detection process to depend on a remote cloud pipeline.</p><p data-end=\"5417\" data-section-id=\"1mpibm6\" data-start=\"5370\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><h2 data-end=\"5417\" data-section-id=\"1mpibm6\" data-start=\"5370\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Edge AI Processing with Raspberry Pi AI HAT+</h2><p data-end=\"5536\" data-start=\"5419\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the centre of the AI processing is the <strong data-end=\"5493\" data-start=\"5461\">Raspberry Pi AI HAT+ 26 TOPS</strong>, powered by Hailo acceleration technology.</p><p data-end=\"5536\" data-start=\"5419\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"5699\" data-start=\"5538\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi/hands-holding-raspberry-pi-ai-hat-board.png \" style=\"width: 800px; height: 600px;\"></p><p data-end=\"5879\" data-start=\"5701\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"5879\" data-start=\"5701\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">The purpose of the accelerator is straightforward: neural-network inference can be assigned to dedicated AI hardware instead of relying only on the Raspberry Pi's main processor.</p><p data-end=\"6110\" data-start=\"5881\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"6110\" data-start=\"5881\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to the presenter, the PPE detection workload is handled by the AI accelerator rather than the Raspberry Pi CPU. The presenter also describes the detection results as appearing together with the camera feed in real time.</p><p data-end=\"6264\" data-start=\"6112\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"6264\" data-start=\"6112\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">These points are useful for understanding the intended architecture, although no frame-rate, latency or processor-utilisation measurements are provided.</p><p data-end=\"6614\" data-start=\"6266\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"6614\" data-start=\"6266\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">From an engineering perspective, using dedicated acceleration is especially useful for computer-vision applications because object detection can become one part of a larger system. The host computer can still be responsible for application logic, visualization and additional integrations while dedicated AI hardware handles the inference workload.</p><p data-end=\"6640\" data-section-id=\"157jd6d\" data-start=\"6616\">&nbsp;</p><h2 data-end=\"6640\" data-section-id=\"157jd6d\" data-start=\"6616\">PPE Detection Results</h2><p data-end=\"6760\" data-start=\"6642\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">The PPE detection output demonstrates how multiple objects and multiple workers can be analysed within the same scene.</p><p data-end=\"6831\" data-start=\"6762\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"6831\" data-start=\"6762\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">In one warehouse scene, a worker wearing a hard hat is detected with:</p><p data-end=\"6850\" data-start=\"6833\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong data-end=\"6850\" data-start=\"6833\">Helmet — 0.88</strong></p><p data-end=\"7018\" data-start=\"6852\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"7018\" data-start=\"6852\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi/warehouse-safety-inspection.png \" style=\"width: 800px; height: 450px;\"></p><p data-end=\"7018\" data-start=\"6852\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"7232\" data-start=\"7020\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">A second scene contains several workers and produces multiple detections simultaneously. Helmet detections appear in approximately the <strong data-end=\"7171\" data-start=\"7155\">0.55 to 0.78</strong> range, while vest detections appear around <strong data-end=\"7231\" data-start=\"7215\">0.65 to 0.74</strong>.</p><p data-end=\"7232\" data-start=\"7020\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"7271\" data-start=\"7234\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">One additional detection is labelled:</p><p data-end=\"7288\" data-start=\"7273\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"7288\" data-start=\"7273\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong data-end=\"7288\" data-start=\"7273\">Head — 0.47</strong></p><p data-end=\"7363\" data-start=\"7290\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"7363\" data-start=\"7290\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, the interface displays a green <strong data-end=\"7352\" data-start=\"7339\">SAFETY OK</strong> indicator.</p><p data-end=\"7596\" data-start=\"7365\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"7596\" data-start=\"7365\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi/safety-ok-at-construction-site.png \" style=\"width: 800px; height: 450px;\"></p><p data-end=\"7659\" data-start=\"7598\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"7659\" data-start=\"7598\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is an important distinction in an AI safety application.</p><p data-end=\"7871\" data-start=\"7661\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"7871\" data-start=\"7661\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Individual bounding boxes tell us <strong data-end=\"7721\" data-start=\"7695\">what the model detects</strong>, while an overall compliance indicator represents the next layer of application logic — deciding what those detections mean for the safety condition.</p><p data-end=\"8197\" data-start=\"7873\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"8197\" data-start=\"7873\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">The exact rule behind the <strong data-end=\"7912\" data-start=\"7899\">SAFETY OK</strong> indicator is not defined here, so the individual detection results are the clearest part of the implementation to evaluate. Even so, the concept is useful: several object-level detections can eventually be translated into one simple decision for an operator or another control system.</p><h2 data-end=\"8241\" data-section-id=\"qqdjbt\" data-start=\"8199\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</h2><h2 data-end=\"8241\" data-section-id=\"qqdjbt\" data-start=\"8199\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">From Detection to Industrial Deployment</h2><p data-end=\"8325\" data-start=\"8243\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">PPE detection is only the first layer of a complete safety-monitoring application.</p><p data-end=\"8365\" data-start=\"8327\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"8365\" data-start=\"8327\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">The current implementation focuses on:</p><p data-end=\"8445\" data-start=\"8367\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"8445\" data-start=\"8367\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong data-end=\"8445\" data-start=\"8367\">Camera input → PPE detection → Confidence score → Compliance visualization</strong></p><p data-end=\"8561\" data-start=\"8447\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"8561\" data-start=\"8447\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">For a real industrial deployment, the next stage would be deciding what happens after the AI produces that result.</p><p data-end=\"8915\" data-start=\"8563\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"8915\" data-start=\"8563\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Depending on the application, the detection status could become an input for a separate integration layer such as event logging, alarms, operator notifications or access-control logic. For example, an entrance-control system could eventually use PPE compliance information as one of the conditions considered before allowing access to a hazardous area.</p><p data-end=\"8999\" data-start=\"8917\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"8999\" data-start=\"8917\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Those functions should be treated separately from the detection demonstrated here.</p><p data-end=\"9233\" data-start=\"9001\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"9233\" data-start=\"9001\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Glove detection is another possible development area. Helmets, vests and gloves are part of the intended PPE requirement, but the current detection results demonstrate helmet and vest recognition rather than a glove detection class.</p><p data-end=\"9391\" data-start=\"9235\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"9391\" data-start=\"9235\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">For deployments that need a more industrial platform around this type of edge AI application, Cytron's <strong data-end=\"9357\" data-start=\"9338\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</strong> provides a relevant path forward.</p><p data-end=\"9628\" data-start=\"9393\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"9628\" data-start=\"9393\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">The IRIV EdgeAI CM5 is an industrial AI controller built around Raspberry Pi Compute Module 5 and supports Raspberry Pi AI acceleration hardware. Cytron also lists <strong data-end=\"9580\" data-start=\"9557\">PPE Zone Monitoring</strong> as one of its supported AI vision applications.</p><p data-end=\"9809\" data-start=\"9630\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"9809\" data-start=\"9630\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Its industrial interfaces, including isolated digital I/O and isolated RS485, can also support integration with other equipment where additional control or signalling is required.</p><p data-end=\"9998\" data-start=\"9811\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"9998\" data-start=\"9811\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">The important distinction is that these are capabilities available for building a larger industrial system. The PPE implementation discussed here focuses on the AI detection layer itself.</p><p data-end=\"10013\" data-section-id=\"8dtpi\" data-start=\"10000\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><h2 data-end=\"10013\" data-section-id=\"8dtpi\" data-start=\"10000\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Conclusion</h2><p data-end=\"10117\" data-start=\"10015\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Edge AI provides a practical way to bring computer vision directly into workplace safety applications.</p><p data-end=\"10354\" data-start=\"10119\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"10354\" data-start=\"10119\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">By combining a camera, Raspberry Pi and dedicated AI accelerator, this PPE detection system can identify helmets and safety vests, provide individual confidence scores and present the results as an easy-to-understand compliance status.</p><p data-end=\"10615\" data-start=\"10356\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"10615\" data-start=\"10356\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">More importantly, the detection result can become the foundation for a much larger safety workflow. PPE recognition can eventually be combined with logging, alerts, operator interfaces or access-control systems depending on the requirements of the deployment.</p><p data-end=\"10895\" data-is-last-node=\"\" data-is-only-node=\"\" data-start=\"10617\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"10895\" data-is-last-node=\"\" data-is-only-node=\"\" data-start=\"10617\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">For teams moving from a proof-of-concept toward an industrial edge AI system, the <strong data-end=\"10718\" data-start=\"10699\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</strong> provides a platform designed for this type of application, combining Raspberry Pi-based computing, AI acceleration and industrial connectivity in a deployment-ready controller.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi/raspberry-pi-for-professional-footer-1.png \" style=\"width: 900px; height: 200px;\"></p><p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;</p><p></p>", "products": []}, "mediapipe-alternative-in-trixie": {"hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/mediapipe-alternative-in-trixie/create-a-youtube-thumbnail-design-in-1280720-169.-title-text-on-thumbnail-must-be-readable-mediapipe-alternatives-on-trixie-subtext-body-skeleton-tracking-main-requirement-important-use.png", "body": "<p></p><h2>Introduction</h2><p>A new operating system, <strong data-end=\"34\" data-start=\"24\">Trixie</strong>, has recently been introduced. However, one important library&nbsp;<strong data-end=\"110\" data-start=\"97\">MediaPipe&nbsp;</strong>is not compatible with it yet. In this tutorial, I’ll introduce an alternative approach you can use to get MediaPipe-like functionality on Trixie.</p><p><br> What You Will Build</p><ul><li>Set up a clean <b>Python 3.11</b> environment on <b>Raspberry Pi OS (Trixie, aarch64)</b> using <b>micromamba</b></li><li>Install <b>tflite-runtime</b> (via piwheels) for running <b>TensorFlow Lite</b> models</li><li>Install <b>OpenCV</b> (via conda-forge) for computer vision features</li><li>Verify everything works by testing imports and checking versions</li><li>Use this setup as a practical alternative when <b>MediaPipe isn’t compatible</b> on Trixie</li></ul><h2>Requirements</h2><p>&nbsp;</p><h3>Hardware:</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/c-raspberry-pi/p-raspberry-pi-5\" target=\"_blank\">Raspberry Pi</a> (aarch64) — recommended <b>Raspberry Pi 5</b></li><li><a href=\"/p-raspberry-pi-a2-microsd\" target=\"_blank\">MicroSD card</a> or <a href=\"/p-raspberry-pi-nvme-ssds-and-kits\" target=\"_blank\">SSD</a> (recommended for faster setup)</li><li>Stable internet connection</li><li>Camera&nbsp;(<a href=\"/p-raspberry-pi-camera-module-3-12mp-with-auto-focus-lens\" target=\"_blank\">Pi Camera</a> / <a href=\"/p-8mp-usb-camera-with-housing-for-raspberry-pi-jetson\" target=\"_blank\">USB camera</a>)</li></ul><h3>Software:</h3><ul><li>Raspberry Pi OS / Debian Trixie (64-bit)</li><li>Terminal access</li><li>micromamba (linux-aarch64)</li><li>Python 3.11 environment (created via micromamba)</li><li>Packages: numpy (&lt;2), tflite-runtime (piwheels), opencv (conda-forge)</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><h2 data-end=\"1416\" data-start=\"1399\">Instructions</h2><h3 data-end=\"1447\" data-start=\"1418\">1) Prepare the Hardware</h3><ol data-end=\"1641\" data-start=\"1448\"><li data-end=\"1523\" data-start=\"1448\"><p data-end=\"1523\" data-start=\"1451\">Insert the <strong data-end=\"1478\" data-start=\"1462\">microSD card</strong> (or connect the SSD if you are using one).</p></li><li data-end=\"1572\" data-start=\"1524\"><p data-end=\"1572\" data-start=\"1527\">Connect the <strong data-end=\"1549\" data-start=\"1539\">camera</strong> to the Raspberry Pi.</p></li><li data-end=\"1641\" data-start=\"1573\"><p data-end=\"1641\" data-start=\"1576\">Power on the Raspberry Pi and wait until it boots successfully.</p></li></ol><hr data-end=\"1646\" data-start=\"1643\"><h3 data-end=\"1684\" data-start=\"1648\">2) Set Up the Operating System</h3><ol data-end=\"1787\" data-start=\"1685\"><li data-end=\"1717\" data-start=\"1685\"><p data-end=\"1717\" data-start=\"1688\">Log in to the Raspberry Pi.</p></li><li data-end=\"1739\" data-start=\"1718\"><p data-end=\"1739\" data-start=\"1721\">Open a terminal.</p></li><li data-end=\"1787\" data-start=\"1740\"><p data-end=\"1787\" data-start=\"1743\">Confirm the system architecture (aarch64):</p></li></ol><p><code>uname -m </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"1829\" data-start=\"1811\">Expected output:</p><p><code>aarch64 </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><ol data-end=\"1901\" data-start=\"1851\" start=\"4\"><li data-end=\"1901\" data-start=\"1851\"><p data-end=\"1901\" data-start=\"1854\">Update and fully upgrade the system (Trixie):</p></li></ol><p><code>sudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt full-upgrade -y </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><ol data-end=\"1992\" data-start=\"1960\" start=\"5\"><li data-end=\"1992\" data-start=\"1960\"><p data-end=\"1992\" data-start=\"1963\">Install basic dependencies:</p></li></ol><p><code>sudo apt install -y curl bzip2 ca-certificates git </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><ol data-end=\"2087\" data-start=\"2058\" start=\"6\"><li data-end=\"2087\" data-start=\"2058\"><p data-end=\"2087\" data-start=\"2061\">Reboot the Raspberry Pi:</p></li></ol><p><code>sudo reboot </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr data-end=\"2117\" data-start=\"2114\"><h3 data-end=\"2162\" data-start=\"2119\">3) Install Micromamba (linux-aarch64)</h3><ol><li data-end=\"2216\" data-start=\"2166\">Download micromamba and extract only the binary:</li></ol><p><code>curl -L https://micro.mamba.pm/api/micromamba/linux-aarch64/latest | tar -xvj bin/micromamba </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><ol start=\"2\"><li>Move micromamba into the system PATH:</li></ol><p><code>sudo mv bin/micromamba /usr/local/bin/ </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><ol data-end=\"2449\" data-start=\"2420\" start=\"3\"><li data-end=\"2449\" data-start=\"2420\"><p data-end=\"2449\" data-start=\"2423\">Verify the installation:</p></li></ol><p><code>micromamba --version </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><ol data-end=\"2530\" data-start=\"2485\" start=\"4\"><li data-end=\"2530\" data-start=\"2485\"><p data-end=\"2530\" data-start=\"2489\">(Optional) View micromamba information:</p></li></ol><p><code>micromamba info </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr data-end=\"2564\" data-start=\"2561\"><h3 data-end=\"2620\" data-start=\"2566\">4) Create and Activate a Python 3.11 Environment</h3><ol data-end=\"2693\" data-start=\"2621\" start=\"1\"><li data-end=\"2693\" data-start=\"2621\"><p data-end=\"2693\" data-start=\"2625\">Initialize the micromamba shell hook for the current Bash session:</p></li></ol><p><code>eval \"$(micromamba shell hook --shell bash)\" </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><ol data-end=\"2819\" data-start=\"2753\" start=\"2\"><li data-end=\"2819\" data-start=\"2753\"><p data-end=\"2819\" data-start=\"2757\">Create a new environment named <font face=\"monospace\">tflite311</font>&nbsp;with Python 3.11:</p></li></ol><p><code>micromamba create -n tflite311 -c conda-forge python=3.11 -y </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><ol data-end=\"2926\" data-start=\"2895\" start=\"3\"><li data-end=\"2926\" data-start=\"2895\"><p data-end=\"2926\" data-start=\"2899\">Activate the environment:</p></li></ol><p><code>micromamba activate tflite311 </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><ol data-end=\"3004\" data-start=\"2971\" start=\"4\"><li data-end=\"3004\" data-start=\"2971\"><p data-end=\"3004\" data-start=\"2975\">Confirm the Python version:</p></li></ol><p><code>python --version </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"3054\" data-start=\"3036\">Expected output:</p><p><code>Python 3.11.x </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr data-end=\"3085\" data-start=\"3082\"><h3 data-end=\"3157\" data-start=\"3087\">5) Install Required Packages (Inside the micromamba Environment)</h3><ol data-end=\"3176\" data-start=\"3158\" start=\"1\"><li data-end=\"3176\" data-start=\"3158\"><p data-end=\"3176\" data-start=\"3162\">Upgrade pip:</p></li></ol><p><code>python -m pip install -U pip </code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><ol data-end=\"3240\" data-start=\"3220\" start=\"2\"><li data-end=\"3240\" data-start=\"3220\"><p data-end=\"3240\" data-start=\"3224\">Install NumPy:</p></li></ol><p data-end=\"3240\" data-start=\"3224\"><code>python -m pip install \"numpy&lt;2\"&nbsp;</code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><ol start=\"3\"><li>Install tflite-runtime&nbsp;from piwheels:</li></ol><pre data-end=\"3428\" data-start=\"3330\"><code>python -m pip install --extra-index-url https://www.piwheels.org/simple tflite-runtime </code></pre><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"3532\" data-start=\"3430\">If successful, you should see an aarch64 build (for example: tflite_runtime-2.14.0 ... aarch64).</p><ol data-end=\"3572\" data-start=\"3534\" start=\"4\"><li data-end=\"3572\" data-start=\"3534\"><p data-end=\"3572\" data-start=\"3538\">Install OpenCV from conda-forge:</p></li></ol><pre data-end=\"3629\" data-start=\"3574\">&nbsp;<code>micromamba install -n tflite311 -c conda-forge opencv -y</code> </pre><p data-end=\"2174\" data-start=\"2086\">&nbsp;</p><hr data-end=\"3634\" data-start=\"3631\"><h3 data-end=\"3695\" data-start=\"3636\">6) Create a Project Folder&nbsp;</h3><ol data-end=\"3762\" data-start=\"3696\" start=\"1\"><li data-end=\"3762\" data-start=\"3696\"><p data-end=\"3762\" data-start=\"3700\">Create and enter a project directory for your MoveNet files:</p></li></ol><p><code>mkdir -p ~/movenet_pose<br> cd ~/movenet_pose</code></p><ol start=\"2\"><li data-end=\"482\" data-start=\"455\">Create your Python scripts:</li></ol><pre data-end=\"535\" data-start=\"484\"><code>nano skeleton_pose.py </code> </pre> <ol start=\"3\"><li data-end=\"575\" data-start=\"542\">Download the TFLite Models&nbsp;</li></ol><p data-end=\"620\" data-start=\"577\">Download MoveNet Lightning (Body Pose)</p><p><code>cd ~/tflite_vision/models<br> wget -O movenet_lightning.tflite https://raw.githubusercontent.com/NSTiwari/Video-Game-Control-using-Pose-Classification-and-TensorFlow-Lite/main/movenet_lightning.tflite<br> ls -lh</code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Run the program</p><p><code>python skeleton_pose.py</code></p><p><font face=\"monospace\">Output:</font></p><p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/mediapipe-alternative-in-trixie/gambar-baru.jpg \" style=\"width: 300px; height: 442px;\"></p><p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align: center;\"><a href=\"https://t.me/raspberrypieducationmy\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/mediapipe-alternative-in-trixie/raspberry-pi-telegram.png \" style=\"width: 800px; height: 178px;\"></a></p><p></p>", "products": [{"name": "Raspberry Pi Camera Module 3 - 12MP with Auto Focus Lens", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-raspberry-pi-camera-module-3-12mp-with-auto-focus-lens", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-RPI-CAM3/V-RPI-CAM3_b-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM112.00++", "oldPrice": null, "qty": "x 1 unit(s)", "stock": null}, {"name": "Raspberry Pi 5 Single Board Computer", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-raspberry-pi-5", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-RASPBERRY-PI-5/v-raspberry-pi-5-main-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM202.00++", "oldPrice": null, "qty": "x 1 unit(s)", "stock": null}, {"name": "Raspberry Pi A2 microSD Card - Pre-loaded with Raspberry Pi OS", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-raspberry-pi-a2-microsd", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-MMR-U-RPIA2/v-mmr-u-rpia2-b-268x268.png", "price": "RM125.00++", "oldPrice": null, "qty": "x 1 unit(s)", "stock": null}, {"name": "Raspberry Pi M.2 HAT+ with NVMe SSDs (Pre-loaded)", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-raspberry-pi-nvme-ssds-and-kits", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-RPI-NVME-SSD/rpi-nv2230-512-a-268x268.png", "price": "RM53.60++", "oldPrice": null, "qty": "x 1 unit(s)", "stock": null}, {"name": "8MP USB Camera with Housing for Raspberry Pi / Jetson", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-8mp-usb-camera-with-housing-for-raspberry-pi-jetson", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/CA-USB-CAM-HS2/ca-usb-cam-hs2-a-268x268.png", "price": "RM195.00", "oldPrice": null, "qty": "x 1 unit(s)", "stock": null}]}, "ai-animal-sound-detection-using-jetson-orin-nx": {"hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/ai-animal-sound-detection-using-jetson-orin-nx/ai-animal-sound-classification-using-jetson-orin-nx.png", "body": "<p></p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Introduction</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An AI-based animal sound classification system can be built on NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX using deep learning and acoustic analysis instead of traditional computer vision methods. This project demonstrates how Artificial Intelligence (AI) can recognise environmental sounds through microphones by analysing audio features in real time. The system is trained using the ESC-50 dataset and can classify three animal sounds: cat, dog, and bird. The setup uses PyTorch with CUDA acceleration on Jetson Orin NX for GPU-based inference and training.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What You Will Build</h2><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Train a real-time animal sound classification model using PyTorch and CUDA</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Detect and classify cat, dog, and bird sounds using microphone input</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Use mel-spectrogram audio processing for AI inference</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Run the application on Jetson Orin NX with GPU acceleration</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Build a simple GUI application for recording and displaying predictions</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Learn how AI can work with sound recognition instead of camera-based vision systems</p></li></ul><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Requirements</h2><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Hardware</h3><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><a href=\"/p-jetson-orin-nx-ai-development-kit\" target=\"_blank\">NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX&nbsp;</a></p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><a href=\"/p-usb-microphone-with-collar-clip\" target=\"_blank\">USB microphone</a></p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Minimum 32GB storage (NVMe or eMMC recommended)</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Stable internet connection during installation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Power adapter capable of stable Jetson operation</p></li></ul><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Software</h3><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ubuntu 22.04 (JetPack 6)</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">CUDA 12.4</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">cuDNN 9.3.0</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">cuDSS 0.7.1</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Python 3.10</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">PyTorch 2.11.0 (aarch64 wheel)</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">librosa</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">sounddevice</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">scikit-learn</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">tkinter</p></li></ul><div class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\" style=\"background: rgb(238, 238, 238); border: 1px solid rgb(204, 204, 204); padding: 5px 10px; text-align: center;\"><strong>GitHub Project Link:</strong>&nbsp; <a href=\"https://github.com/itswinzie/animal-sound-ai\" target=\"_blank\"><strong class=\"mr-2 flex-self-stretch d-none d-md-block no-wrap overflow-x-hidden\" itemprop=\"name\">Animal-Sound-AI&nbsp;</strong></a></div><div class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\" style=\"background: rgb(238, 238, 238); border: 1px solid rgb(204, 204, 204); padding: 5px 10px; text-align: center;\"><strong>ESC-50 Dataset:</strong> <a class=\"underline underline underline-offset-2 decoration-1 decoration-current/40 hover:decoration-current focus:decoration-current\" href=\"https://github.com/karolpiczak/ESC-50\" target=\"_blank\">ESC-50 GitHub</a><br> <strong>ESC-50 Author:</strong> <a href=\"https://github.com/karolpiczak\" target=\"_blank\">Karol Piczak</a></div><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instructions</h2><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1) Verify JetPack Installation</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Before installing dependencies, verify the installed JetPack and CUDA versions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><code class=\"language-bash\">cat /etc/nv_tegra_release</code></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Expected output:</p><pre style=\"text-align: justify;\"><code class=\"language-bash\">R36 (release), REVISION: 5.0, BOARD: t234 </code></pre><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Check CUDA version:</p><pre style=\"text-align: justify;\"><code class=\"language-bash\">nvcc --version </code></pre><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Expected:</p><pre style=\"text-align: justify;\"><code class=\"language-bash\">Cuda compilation tools, release 12.4 </code></pre><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If CUDA is not detected:</p><pre style=\"text-align: justify;\"><code class=\"language-bash\">export PATH=/usr/local/cuda/bin:$PATH </code></pre><hr><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2) Check Existing Libraries</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Verify cuDNN and cuDSS libraries.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><code class=\"language-bash\">find /usr -name 'libcudnn.so*' 2&gt;/dev/null<br> find / -name 'libcudss*' 2&gt;/dev/null<br> echo $LD_LIBRARY_PATH</code></p><hr><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3) Install CUDA, cuDNN and cuDSS</h3><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">CUDA 12.4</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">CUDA is already included with JetPack 6.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Verify CUDA:</p><pre style=\"text-align: justify;\"><code class=\"language-bash\">nvcc --version ls /usr/local/cuda/lib64/ </code></pre><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Add CUDA paths permanently:</p><pre style=\"text-align: justify;\"><code class=\"language-bash\">echo 'export PATH=/usr/local/cuda/bin:$PATH' &gt;&gt; ~/.bashrc echo 'export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/local/cuda/lib64:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH' &gt;&gt; ~/.bashrc source ~/.bashrc </code></pre><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pytorch</h3><p data-end=\"196\" data-start=\"0\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Go to the official NVIDIA PyPI website at <a data-end=\"118\" data-start=\"42\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_new\">PyPI Jetson AI Lab</a>, or simply click the download&nbsp; below to automatically start the download.</p><p data-end=\"196\" data-start=\"0\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p style=\"margin-left: 40px;\"><a href=\"https://pypi.jetson-ai-lab.io/jp6/cu126/+f/46b/b8b13f844b211/torch-2.11.0-cp310-cp310-linux_aarch64.whl#sha256=46bb8b13f844b2111f6f41abc703d01075a7185e0c587cb002e957ede2b8c8a3\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><input style=\"font-size: 16px; padding: 8px 16px;\" type=\"button\" value=\"Download PyTorch\"> </a></p><p style=\"margin-left: 40px;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"365\" data-start=\"226\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">After the download is complete, open your file manager and search for the PyTorch file.<br data-end=\"316\" data-start=\"313\"> Right-click the file and select <strong data-end=\"364\" data-start=\"348\">Copy as Path</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Then install PyTorch using:<br> <code>pip install <span style=\"color:#27ae60;\">\"paste-your-path-here\"</span></code></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Example:<br> <code>pip install <span style=\"color:#27ae60\">your_pytorch_file.whl</span></code></p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">cuDNN 9.3.0</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Verify cuDNN:</p><pre style=\"text-align: justify;\"><code class=\"language-bash\">dpkg -l | grep cudnn find /usr -name 'libcudnn.so*' </code></pre><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Install if missing:</p><pre style=\"text-align: justify;\"><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo apt update sudo apt install libcudnn9 libcudnn9-dev </code></pre><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">cuDSS 0.7.1</h3><p data-end=\"37\" data-start=\"0\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Download cuDSS from NVIDIA Developer.</p><p data-end=\"91\" data-start=\"39\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Go to the official NVIDIA website to download cuDSS: <a data-end=\"181\" data-start=\"93\" href=\"https://developer.nvidia.com/cudss?utm_source=chatgpt.com\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_new\">NVIDIA cuDSS Developer Page</a></p><p data-end=\"259\" data-start=\"183\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Or simply click the download below to automatically start the download.</p><p data-end=\"259\" data-start=\"183\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"398\" data-start=\"261\" style=\"margin-left: 40px;\"><a href=\"https://developer.download.nvidia.com/compute/cudss/0.7.1/local_installers/cudss-local-tegra-repo-ubuntu2204-0.7.1_0.7.1-1_arm64.deb\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><input style=\"font-size: 16px; padding: 8px 16px;\" type=\"button\" value=\"Download cuDSS\"></a>&nbsp;</p><p data-end=\"398\" data-start=\"261\" style=\"margin-left: 40px;\">&nbsp;</p><p>After the download is complete, open your file manager and search for the cuDSS file.<br> Right-click the file and select Copy as Path.</p><p>Then install the package using: &nbsp;</p><p><code>sudo dpkg -i <span style=\"color:#27ae60;\">\"paste-the-copied-path-here\"</span></code></p><p>Example: &nbsp;</p><p><code>sudo dpkg -i <span style=\"color:#27ae60;\">\"/home/orin_nx/Downloads/example.deb\"</span></code></p><p>Copy the keyring:</p><p><code>sudo cp /var/cudss-local-tegra-repo-ubuntu2204-0.7.1/cudss-*-keyring.gpg /usr/share/keyrings/</code></p><p>Update the package list:</p><p><code>sudo apt-get update</code></p><p>Finally, install cuDSS:</p><p><code>sudo apt-get -y install cudss</code></p><p>&nbsp;</p><h3>Verify PyTorch and CUDA:</h3><p>After complete all intallation, need to verify for all installation</p><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">python3 -c \" import torch print('PyTorch Version :', torch.__version__) print('CUDA Available &nbsp; :', torch.cuda.is_available()) print('CUDA Version &nbsp; &nbsp; :', torch.version.cuda) print('Device Name &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;:', torch.cuda.get_device_name(0) if torch.cuda.is_available() else 'N/A') \" </code></pre><p>Expected output:</p><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">PyTorch Version : 2.11.0 CUDA Available &nbsp;: True CUDA Version &nbsp; &nbsp;: 12.6 Device Name &nbsp; &nbsp; : Orin</code></pre><hr><h3>4) Create Project Folder</h3><p>Download the main project directory.</p><p>Project structure:</p><div style=\"background:#eeeeee;border:1px solid #cccccc;padding:5px 10px;\"><tt>voice/<br> ├── train.py&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; # AI model training script using ESC-50 dataset<br> ├── main_gui.py&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;# Main GUI application for sound detection<br> ├── model.pth&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;# Trained PyTorch model file<br> ├── ESC-50/&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;# ESC-50 dataset folder (submodule — karolpiczak/ESC-50)<br> │&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; ├── audio/&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;# Audio files (.wav)<br> │&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; └── meta/&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; # Dataset metadata and labels<br> └── venv/&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;# Python virtual environment</tt></div><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Clone the GitHub</p><p><code>git clone&nbsp;https://github.com/itswinzie/animal-sound-ai.git<br> cd animal-sound-ai</code></p><p><strong>Note:</strong> This download already includes the ESC-50 dataset, so there is no need to install ESC-50 separately. The tutorial for manual ESC-50 installation is provided <a href=\"/index.php?route=eblog/epost&amp;epost_id=2053&amp;r=0&amp;no_cache#:~:text=Download%20ESC%2D50%20Dataset\">below for reference only.</a></p><hr><h3>5) Create Python Virtual Environment</h3><p><code class=\"language-bash\">python3 -m venv venv&nbsp;&nbsp;<br> source venv/bin/activate</code></p><p>Upgrade pip:</p><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">pip install --upgrade pip </code> </pre><hr><h3>6) Install Python Dependencies</h3><p>Install system packages:</p><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo apt update sudo apt install -y \\ libportaudio2 \\ libportaudio-dev \\ ffmpeg \\ python3-tk \\ libsndfile1 </code></pre><p>Install Python packages:</p><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">pip install \\ numpy&gt;=1.21.0 \\ librosa&gt;=0.9.0 \\ sounddevice&gt;=0.4.5 \\ scikit-learn&gt;=1.0.0 </code></pre><p>Create <tt>requirements.txt</tt>:</p><pre><code class=\"language-text\">numpy&gt;=1.21.0 librosa&gt;=0.9.0 sounddevice&gt;=0.4.5 scikit-learn&gt;=1.0.0 </code> </pre><hr><h3>7) Train the AI Model</h3><p>The system uses a CNN-based audio classifier trained on mel-spectrograms.</p><p>Training configuration:</p><ul><li><p>Epochs: 80</p></li><li><p>Batch size: 16</p></li><li><p>Optimiser: AdamW</p></li><li><p>Loss: CrossEntropyLoss</p></li><li><p>Classes: Dog, Cat, Bird</p></li></ul><p>Run training:</p><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">cd ~/voice source venv/bin/activate python3 train.py </code></pre><p>Example output:</p><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">Epoch 01/80 Loss: 1.0532 Train: 38.5% Val: 62.5% Epoch 05/80 Loss: 0.5838 Train: 78.1% Val: 87.5% Epoch 07/80 Loss: 0.4848 Train: 80.2% Val: 95.8% </code></pre><p>Expected final output:</p><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">Done! Best accuracy: 95.8% Model saved: model.pth </code></pre><h3>&nbsp;</h3><hr><h3>8) Launch the GUI Application</h3><p>Run the application:</p><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">cd ~/voice source venv/bin/activate python3 main_gui.py </code></pre><p>Expected startup output:</p><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">Model loaded : ['Dog', 'Cat', 'Bird'] Device : CUDA Record time : 5s </code></pre><hr><h3>9) How to Use the Application</h3><ul><li><p>Press and hold the button to start recording</p></li><li><p>Release the button to stop recording</p></li><li><p>AI classification runs automatically</p></li><li><p>Top predictions and confidence scores are displayed</p></li><li><p>Detection history is saved in the GUI</p></li></ul><hr><h2>Result</h2><p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/ai-animal-sound-detection-using-jetson-orin-nx/6330348776161218826.jpg \" style=\"width: 650px; height: 781px;\"></p><h2>Download ESC-50 Dataset</h2><h3>Option A — Kaggle</h3><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">pip install kaggle kaggle datasets download -d mmoreaux/environmental-sound-classification-50 unzip environmental-sound-classification-50.zip -d ~/voice/ESC-50 </code></pre><h3>Option B — GitHub</h3><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">git clone https://github.com/karolpiczak/ESC-50.git ~/voice/ESC-50 </code></pre><p>Verify dataset:</p><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">ls ~/voice/ESC-50/audio/ | wc -l head -3 ~/voice/ESC-50/meta/esc50.csv </code></pre><p>Expected:</p><pre><code class=\"language-text\">filename,fold,target,category,esc10,src_file,take</code></pre><hr><h2>How the System Works</h2><h3>Audio Pipeline</h3><ol><li><p>Microphone records live audio</p></li><li><p>Audio is converted into mel-spectrograms</p></li><li><p>CNN model processes spectrogram features</p></li><li><p>AI predicts the animal category</p></li><li><p>Results are displayed in the GUI</p></li></ol><h3>Supported Classes</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Dataset Category</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dog</td><td>dog</td></tr><tr><td>Cat</td><td>cat</td></tr><tr><td>Bird</td><td>crow</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Note: ESC-50 uses “crow” as the closest bird category.</p><h2>Performance Notes</h2><ul><li><p>Jetson GPU acceleration significantly improves training speed</p></li><li><p>CUDA inference runs in the main thread for stability</p></li><li><p>Audio recording runs in a separate thread</p></li><li><p>Training takes approximately 15–25 minutes on Jetson Orin NX</p></li></ul><h2>Common Problems</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ImportError: libcudnn.so.8</td><td>Wrong PyTorch wheel</td><td>Use torch 2.11.0 wheel</td></tr><tr><td>ImportError: libcudss.so.0</td><td>cuDSS missing</td><td>Install libcudss0</td></tr><tr><td>CUDA: False</td><td>CUDA path missing</td><td>Fix LD_LIBRARY_PATH</td></tr><tr><td>Segmentation fault</td><td>CUDA used in background thread</td><td>Run inference in main thread</td></tr><tr><td>model.pth missing</td><td>Model not trained</td><td>Run train.py</td></tr><tr><td>0 samples found</td><td>Wrong dataset path</td><td>Verify ESC-50 location</td></tr><tr><td>PortAudio error</td><td>Microphone issue</td><td>Check devices using sounddevice</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Quick Command Reference</h2><h3>Setup</h3><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">source ~/voice/venv/bin/activate sudo dpkg -i ~/Downloads/cudss-local-tegra-repo-ubuntu2204-0.7.1_0.7.1-1_arm64.deb sudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt install libcudss0 pip install ~/Downloads/torch-2.11.0-cp310-cp310-linux_aarch64.whl pip install numpy librosa sounddevice scikit-learn cd ~/voice &amp;&amp; python3 train.py </code></pre><h3>Daily Usage</h3><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">source ~/voice/venv/bin/activate cd ~/voice &amp;&amp; python3 main_gui.py </code></pre><h3>Diagnostics</h3><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">nvcc --version find /usr -name 'libcudnn.so*' find / -name 'libcudss.so*' 2&gt;/dev/null python3 -c \"import torch; print(torch.cuda.is_available())\" python3 -m sounddevice</code></pre><hr><p style=\"text-align: center;\"><a href=\"https://t.me/nvidiajetson_cytron\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/ai-animal-sound-detection-using-jetson-orin-nx/nvidia-jetson-telegram.png \" style=\"width: 900px; height: 200px;\"></a></p><p></p>", "products": [{"name": "Jetson Orin NX AI Development Bundle with 16GB", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-jetson-orin-nx-ai-development-kit", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/JN-ORNX-16G-DK/jn-ornx-16g-dk-a-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM7,770.00", "oldPrice": null, "qty": "x 1 unit(s)", "stock": null}, {"name": "USB Microphone with Collar Clip", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-usb-microphone-with-collar-clip", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/CA-USB-MIC/ca-usb-mic-a-268x268.png", "price": "RM25.00", "oldPrice": null, "qty": "x 1 unit(s)", "stock": null}]}};